  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Thursday: The expected shortwave is currently \nalong coastline and slowly making its way into the Cascades. It \nwill start to push high clouds across the region by the evening.\nPrecip chances will start to increase along the Cascade crest \ntonight and spread eastward through Thursday. There is not a lot\nof moisture associated with the wave as probability of at least\n0.1 of an inch is along the Cascade Crest. The rest of the \nregion has a chance to receive at least a few drops. The Basin \nand northern mountain precip chances range from 20-40%. The \nIdaho Panhandle has a 40-60% chance. Amounts are expected be \nless than 0.1 of an inch. Snow levels dip to around 4500ft \ntonight. Precip along the crest will mainly be snow or mix. Snow\naccumulations are not expected to be more than inch. On \nThursday, winds will be breezy across the Basin with gusts into \nthe low 20 mph range. Highs will be in the 40s to low 50s. \nOvernight lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s.\n\nFriday and Wednesday: The trough will press through the region.\nFriday trends drier with light precip chances across the \nCascades and north WA. Precip chances range from 10-20% as \nmoisture is not expected much more than a trace. A more robust \nwave will push through the region late starting Friday night and\nlasting into Sunday. Precipitable water amounts with this wave \nis 0.6-0.8 of an inch. With snow levels around 3500-4500 feet, \nCascades passes are expected to get light snow through Sunday. \nWashington Pass has a 25-75 probability of 1 to 4 inches. \nStevens is less with a Trace to 1 inch. Sprinkles are possible \nacross the rest of the region. Winds will be also be breezy as \nthe wave is being supported by a decent upper level jet. Gusts \nhave potential to reach into the 30 mph range. Highs continue in\nthe upper 40s to low 50s. Overnight lows stay in the upper 20s \nand 30s.\n\nMonday will be brief break in the pattern as the shallow ridge \ndevelops behind the trough. Starting Tuesday, a weak atmospheric\nriver will start to press into the Pacific Northwest. The \nimpacts will mainly be over the Cascades. It currently has a \nspread of 0.5-1 inch. The rest of the region has a spread of \n0.1-0.5 of an inch through Wednesday. The warmer air will bring \nsnow levels back to around 6000ft, keeping mainly a rain event \nacross the region. Highs will be in the upper 40s to 50s. \nOvernight lows stay in the 30s and low 40s. /JDC",
